WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.376 --> 00:00:17.180
The morning, Tom and I got up very early and went down to the kitchen.

2
00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:23.041
We patted the dogs by the back door, and we talked to the black servant who had used

3
00:00:23.081 --> 00:00:24.182
the key the night before.

4
00:00:25.302 --> 00:00:29.543
We watched him fixing a tray of food, and we asked if it was for the dogs.

5
00:00:29.563 --> 00:00:32.744
"No," he said with a smile.

6
00:00:33.312 --> 00:00:34.693
"Would you like to see who it's for?"

7
00:00:34.714 --> 00:00:39.878
"Yes," said Tom, and we followed the servant out to the shed.

8
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:43.802
As soon as he unlocked the door, we ran inside.

9
00:00:44.683 --> 00:00:46.645
Jim's leg was chained to the bed.

10
00:00:47.546 --> 00:00:51.489
He recognized us right away, but I made a sign for him to be quiet.

11
00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:54.572
I didn't want anyone to guess that we knew him.

12
00:00:55.854 --> 00:00:58.096
The servant put down the tray and left.

13
00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:03.504
We just had a moment to whisper to Jim that we were going to set him free and

14
00:01:03.664 --> 00:01:04.644
then we had to leave.

15
00:01:05.585 --> 00:01:09.548
It was still too early for breakfast, so Tom and I went into the woods.

16
00:01:10.529 --> 00:01:15.673
We gathered some chunks of rotting wood with glowing fungus that people call "foxfire."

17
00:01:17.515 --> 00:01:20.617
We could use them to give us a little light while we dug at night.

18
00:01:20.637 --> 00:01:24.260
"A lantern would be too bright," said Tom.

19
00:01:25.201 --> 00:01:28.964
I was happy that our plan was going so well, but Tom was not.

20
00:01:29.716 --> 00:01:32.237
"There ought to be more to do," he said.

21
00:01:32.257 --> 00:01:38.040
"I wish Uncle Silas had put a guard on the shed door because then we could drug him."

22
00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:40.161
Then he smiled.

23
00:01:40.181 --> 00:01:41.861
"I know what we can do.

24
00:01:43.002 --> 00:01:47.384
We can make a ladder out of torn sheets and take it to Jim hidden inside a big

25
00:01:47.424 --> 00:01:51.786
pie. He can hide the ladder in his mattress until he needs it."

26
00:01:51.806 --> 00:01:54.927
"But he doesn't need a ladder," I said.

27
00:01:54.947 --> 00:01:56.548
"We're going to dig him out."

28
00:01:58.312 --> 00:02:04.435
"In a book I read once about escapes, there was a ladder hidden in a pie," retorted Tom.

29
00:02:05.395 --> 00:02:07.216
"We have to do things the right way."

30
00:02:08.056 --> 00:02:11.898
He told me to take a sheet from the clothesline when Aunt Sally wasn't looking.

31
00:02:11.918 --> 00:02:16.860
"And take a shirt too," he said, "for Jim to write a journal on.

32
00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:19.561
Prisoners always keep journals."

33
00:02:21.282 --> 00:02:25.884
After breakfast, when no one was in the yard, I borrowed the sheet and the shirt.

34
00:02:26.924 --> 00:02:31.847
Tom and I put them in an old sack along with the foxfire and took the sack into

35
00:02:31.887 --> 00:02:32.208
the hut.

36
00:02:33.168 --> 00:02:36.350
"Now all we need is something to dig with," said Tom.

37
00:02:37.271 --> 00:02:38.952
"Look around us," I said.

38
00:02:38.972 --> 00:02:40.873
"There are picks and shovels here."

39
00:02:42.435 --> 00:02:44.016
Tom looked at me with contempt.

40
00:02:45.096 --> 00:02:48.799
"When heroes are trying to escape in books, they never have shovels."

41
00:02:48.819 --> 00:02:52.241
"Well, if we don't use shovels, what do we use?"

42
00:02:53.022 --> 00:02:53.402
I asked.

43
00:02:53.422 --> 00:02:56.304
"Pocketknives," said Tom.

44
00:02:57.164 --> 00:02:59.745
"We're going to dig under that shed with pocketknives?"

45
00:03:00.565 --> 00:03:01.925
I asked incredulously.

46
00:03:01.945 --> 00:03:04.046
"That's crazy, Tom!"

47
00:03:04.586 --> 00:03:06.347
I was starting to become annoyed with him.

48
00:03:06.367 --> 00:03:13.229
"I read a book once about a man who dug himself out of a castle—it took him 37

49
00:03:13.289 --> 00:03:14.989
years," said Tom.

50
00:03:15.009 --> 00:03:18.630
"We can't take that long," I protested.

51
00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:23.892
"Uncle Silas has written to New Orleans to tell the plantation he has their runaway slave.

52
00:03:25.136 --> 00:03:27.958
Soon he'll get a letter explaining that Jim isn't from there."

53
00:03:29.018 --> 00:03:31.540
Tom paused to think and then smiled at me.

54
00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:34.981
"We'll just have to dig faster," he said.

55
00:03:35.682 --> 00:03:37.463
It did no good to argue with Tom.

56
00:03:39.824 --> 00:03:43.646
So I borrowed some pocketknives from the house, as he asked me to.

57
00:03:44.627 --> 00:03:50.390
That night, after everyone was asleep, we went to the hut and started digging with the knives.

58
00:03:51.691 --> 00:03:54.012
The hut was right next to Jim's shed.

59
00:03:54.592 --> 00:03:56.673
We planned to come up under Jim's bed.

60
00:03:57.733 --> 00:03:59.374
We dug until about midnight.

61
00:03:59.994 --> 00:04:03.136
Our hole wasn't very deep, and our hands were blistered.

62
00:04:04.897 --> 00:04:11.279
At last Tom sighed and said, "I hate to do it, but I think we'll have to use

63
00:04:11.319 --> 00:04:11.900
the shovels.

64
00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:15.021
We can pretend that we're using the knives."

65
00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:21.184
The next night, using the tools in the hut, we dug all the way through to Jim's shed.

66
00:04:22.184 --> 00:04:24.585
We lit a candle that we had borrowed from the house.

67
00:04:27.045 --> 00:04:30.566
Jim was so relieved to see us that he hugged us and almost cried.

68
00:04:31.587 --> 00:04:34.207
He wanted us to take him out of his prison right away.

69
00:04:35.348 --> 00:04:40.549
I looked at Jim's chain and saw that we could free him if we lifted his bed and

70
00:04:40.669 --> 00:04:42.570
slipped the chain off the leg of the bed.

71
00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:47.611
I wanted to do it, but Tom told Jim that we had other things to do first.

72
00:04:48.791 --> 00:04:51.312
Jim told us that he thought it would be all right to wait.

73
00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:57.624
He said that Aunt Sally and Uncle Silas visited him every day to make sure that he was

74
00:04:57.684 --> 00:05:03.067
comfortable. He was getting decent food, enough water, and clean sheets from the servants.

75
00:05:04.488 --> 00:05:08.691
Tom explained that we would be sending Jim a pie with a ladder hidden inside.

76
00:05:09.992 --> 00:05:13.154
He also gave Jim the shirt I had borrowed from the clothesline.

77
00:05:14.315 --> 00:05:17.177
He told Jim that he should write a journal on it with his blood.

78
00:05:17.197 --> 00:05:24.988
"Tom, you're a good boy," said Jim, "but these things make no sense to me."

79
00:05:25.008 --> 00:05:26.049
I tried to explain.

80
00:05:26.069 --> 00:05:29.572
"Tom wants us to have an adventure, just like the ones in books.

81
00:05:30.973 --> 00:05:33.396
But trust me, Jim, we know how to free you.

82
00:05:34.136 --> 00:05:36.779
If you are in any danger, we'll get you out right away."

83
00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:40.602
Tom's thirst for adventure was making me nervous.

84
00:05:41.643 --> 00:05:46.028
But I was so grateful to him for helping that I went along with his wild schemes.
